{
 "fps": 30,
 "lead": 0.8,
 "tail": 2.0,
 "footer": "Sources: canada.ca \u00b7 CRA CPP contribution rates, maximums and exemptions; EI premium rates and maximums (2026); T4127 payroll formulas (2026) \u00b7 Examples, general info, not advice",
 "vo": [
  {
   "id": "L1",
   "file": "vo/L1.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Dana's first paycheque of the year: two thousand, five hundred and fifty-two dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L2",
   "file": "vo/L2.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Her last paycheque of the year: two thousand, eight hundred and six."
  },
  {
   "id": "L3",
   "file": "vo/L3.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Same salary. Same job. No raise. Two hundred and fifty-four dollars more, every two weeks, at the end of the year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L4",
   "file": "vo/L4.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "It isn't a mistake. So where does it come from? And why does it disappear again in January?"
  },
  {
   "id": "L5",
   "file": "vo/L5.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "By the end of this video, you'll be able to read every line on your pay stub, and you'll know exactly when your paycheque gets bigger."
  },
  {
   "id": "L6",
   "file": "vo/L6.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "All numbers: twenty twenty-six, Ontario, a ninety thousand dollar salary, paid every two weeks. Examples, not real people. General information, not advice."
  },
  {
   "id": "L7",
   "file": "vo/L7.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "First: the four lines."
  },
  {
   "id": "L8",
   "file": "vo/L8.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Dana earns ninety thousand dollars a year. Twenty-six paydays. Each one starts at three thousand, four hundred and sixty-one dollars and fifty-four cents."
  },
  {
   "id": "L9",
   "file": "vo/L9.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Then three things come off. C P P, the Canada Pension Plan: one hundred and ninety-seven dollars and ninety-five cents."
  },
  {
   "id": "L10",
   "file": "vo/L10.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "E I, Employment Insurance: fifty-six dollars and forty-two cents."
  },
  {
   "id": "L11",
   "file": "vo/L11.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And income tax, federal and Ontario together: about six hundred and fifty-five dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L12",
   "file": "vo/L12.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "What's left, two thousand, five hundred and fifty-two dollars, lands in her account."
  },
  {
   "id": "L13",
   "file": "vo/L13.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Two of those lines have a limit. One doesn't. That one difference explains the fall paycheque."
  },
  {
   "id": "L14",
   "file": "vo/L14.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now, C P P."
  },
  {
   "id": "L15",
   "file": "vo/L15.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "In twenty twenty-six, C P P is five point nine five percent of your pay, after a small exemption: the first three thousand, five hundred dollars of the year aren't charged."
  },
  {
   "id": "L16",
   "file": "vo/L16.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "It stops at a ceiling: seventy-four thousand, six hundred dollars of earnings. The most an employee pays is four thousand, two hundred and thirty dollars and forty-five cents."
  },
  {
   "id": "L17",
   "file": "vo/L17.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Then there's a second layer, called C P P two. Four percent on earnings between seventy-four thousand, six hundred and eighty-five thousand dollars. At most, four hundred and sixteen dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L18",
   "file": "vo/L18.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And here's a detail most people never see: part of your C P P is a deduction on your tax return, and part of it gives you a tax credit. So C P P also lowers your income tax a little."
  },
  {
   "id": "L19",
   "file": "vo/L19.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now, E I."
  },
  {
   "id": "L20",
   "file": "vo/L20.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "In twenty twenty-six, E I is one point six three percent of your pay, outside Quebec."
  },
  {
   "id": "L21",
   "file": "vo/L21.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "It stops at sixty-eight thousand, nine hundred dollars of earnings. The most an employee pays is one thousand, one hundred and twenty-three dollars and seven cents."
  },
  {
   "id": "L22",
   "file": "vo/L22.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "E I is insurance. It's what pays benefits if you lose your job, have a baby, or get sick, if you qualify."
  },
  {
   "id": "L23",
   "file": "vo/L23.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now, income tax."
  },
  {
   "id": "L24",
   "file": "vo/L24.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Income tax has no ceiling. Your employer estimates your yearly tax from your pay and your T D one form, and spreads it across every paycheque."
  },
  {
   "id": "L25",
   "file": "vo/L25.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "For Dana, that's about six hundred and fifty-five dollars every payday, all year long."
  },
  {
   "id": "L26",
   "file": "vo/L26.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Payroll treats each paycheque as if you'd earn the same amount all year. So if your pay changes mid-year, or a bonus lands, the tax line changes with it."
  },
  {
   "id": "L27",
   "file": "vo/L27.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And if the estimate is off, your tax return settles the difference: a refund, or a balance owing. That was our last video."
  },
  {
   "id": "L28",
   "file": "vo/L28.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "So C P P and E I stop. Income tax doesn't. Now watch what happens to Dana's paycheque in the fall."
  },
  {
   "id": "L29",
   "file": "vo/L29.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Here's Dana's year, paycheque by paycheque."
  },
  {
   "id": "L30",
   "file": "vo/L30.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "By paycheque number twenty, her earnings pass sixty-eight thousand, nine hundred dollars. E I takes only a partial amount, and then stops for the year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L31",
   "file": "vo/L31.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Paycheque twenty-one: no E I. Her pay goes up by about fifty-six dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L32",
   "file": "vo/L32.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Paycheque twenty-two: she passes seventy-four thousand, six hundred. Regular C P P stops. But C P P two starts."
  },
  {
   "id": "L33",
   "file": "vo/L33.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "For the next few paycheques, C P P two takes four percent: up to one hundred and thirty-eight dollars and forty-six cents each time."
  },
  {
   "id": "L34",
   "file": "vo/L34.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Then, around eighty-five thousand dollars, C P P two stops too."
  },
  {
   "id": "L35",
   "file": "vo/L35.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Paycheque twenty-six: no C P P. No E I. Only income tax. Two thousand, eight hundred and six dollars and forty cents."
  },
  {
   "id": "L36",
   "file": "vo/L36.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "That's the fall raise. Two hundred and fifty-four dollars more than in January."
  },
  {
   "id": "L37",
   "file": "vo/L37.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "But it isn't a raise. Dana already paid the full year of C P P and E I earlier. And on January first, the limits reset."
  },
  {
   "id": "L38",
   "file": "vo/L38.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Dana's first paycheque of the new year drops back to about where it started. If you didn't know that was coming, January feels like a pay cut."
  },
  {
   "id": "L39",
   "file": "vo/L39.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now, the half you never see."
  },
  {
   "id": "L40",
   "file": "vo/L40.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Your employer pays too. For every dollar of C P P Dana pays, her employer pays the same amount. That's another four thousand, six hundred and forty-six dollars and forty-five cents."
  },
  {
   "id": "L41",
   "file": "vo/L41.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "For E I, the employer pays one point four times what Dana pays. One thousand, five hundred and seventy-two dollars and thirty cents."
  },
  {
   "id": "L42",
   "file": "vo/L42.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Together, that's about six thousand, two hundred and nineteen dollars a year, on top of her salary, that never shows on her stub."
  },
  {
   "id": "L43",
   "file": "vo/L43.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "So a ninety thousand dollar job really costs the employer about ninety-six thousand, two hundred dollars. Worth knowing the next time you compare an employee offer with a contract."
  },
  {
   "id": "L44",
   "file": "vo/L44.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now, the full year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L45",
   "file": "vo/L45.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Income tax: about seventeen thousand and thirty-four dollars. C P P and C P P two: four thousand, six hundred and forty-six dollars. E I: one thousand, one hundred and twenty-three."
  },
  {
   "id": "L46",
   "file": "vo/L46.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "In total, about twenty-two thousand, eight hundred dollars come off ninety thousand. Dana keeps about sixty-seven thousand, one hundred and ninety-seven."
  },
  {
   "id": "L47",
   "file": "vo/L47.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Where this changes."
  },
  {
   "id": "L48",
   "file": "vo/L48.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Earn less than sixty-eight thousand, nine hundred, and you never hit the limits: C P P and E I come off every paycheque, all year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L49",
   "file": "vo/L49.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Work two jobs, and each employer takes C P P and E I separately. If you pay more than the yearly maximum, the extra comes back on your tax return."
  },
  {
   "id": "L50",
   "file": "vo/L50.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Self-employed? You pay both halves of C P P yourself, and E I is optional. And in Quebec, the rules are different."
  },
  {
   "id": "L51",
   "file": "vo/L51.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Here's your checklist."
  },
  {
   "id": "L52",
   "file": "vo/L52.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "One. On your stub, find the year-to-date totals for C P P and E I."
  },
  {
   "id": "L53",
   "file": "vo/L53.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Two. Compare them with the yearly maximums. That tells you when your fall raise starts."
  },
  {
   "id": "L54",
   "file": "vo/L54.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Three. Plan for January, when the deductions come back."
  },
  {
   "id": "L55",
   "file": "vo/L55.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Four. Two jobs? Check your tax return for a C P P or E I overpayment."
  },
  {
   "id": "L56",
   "file": "vo/L56.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "So, what did we learn?"
  },
  {
   "id": "L57",
   "file": "vo/L57.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "One. Your pay stub has four lines: gross pay, C P P, E I and income tax."
  },
  {
   "id": "L58",
   "file": "vo/L58.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Two. C P P and E I have yearly limits. Income tax doesn't."
  },
  {
   "id": "L59",
   "file": "vo/L59.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Three. The fall raise is real money, but it's only timing. January takes it back."
  },
  {
   "id": "L60",
   "file": "vo/L60.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Four. Your employer pays as much C P P as you do, and more E I."
  },
  {
   "id": "L61",
   "file": "vo/L61.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Next on this channel: the R R S P with real numbers, and when it actually reduces your tax. Subscribe, so you don't miss it."
  }
